<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taruga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th–3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taruga is an archaeological site of the Nok culture in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, about 60 kilometres south-east of Abuja, known for terracotta figurines and early iron working. A trial excavation of eight days in January 1961 found the bases and walls of smelting furnaces still in place, with iron slag, fragments of tuyères, pottery and figurine fragments. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-fagg">Bernard Fagg</a>'s excavations there in the 1960s gave radiocarbon dates of about the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE, among the earliest for iron working in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taruga.jpg" alt="Taruga" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Verbreitung_Nok-Kultur-en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Taruga lies in the Middle Belt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and is one of many sites in the centre of the country where objects of the Nok culture have been excavated. Since 1945 similar figurines and pottery have been found across the area, often uncovered by chance by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> miners, with dates from before 500 BCE to 200 CE. The region was probably moister and more heavily wooded then than it is today, though still north of the zone of dense forest, and its people would have lived by farming and raising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>.</p><p>As the climate slowly became drier, the people of the Nok culture may have moved south. On this view they could be among the ancestors of peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igala">Igala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> and Ibo, whose art shows likenesses to the earlier Nok works.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The early terracotta figurines of Taruga are decorated with bands of oblique comb stamping, parallel grooves, false relief chevrons and incised hatched triangles, designs that appear to have influenced the later traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a>. Later styles resemble finds from Rim in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-volta">Upper Volta</a>, with twisted and carved roulettes. Many of the heads and busts may once have belonged to whole figures, which probably represented heroes and ancestors and were kept in shrines in village compounds. Pots were covered with raised dots made by a carved roulette, often joined by grooved lines in a net-like pattern. Both figurines and pottery were fired at low temperature and are fragile.</p><p>The furnaces bear on an open question. It has been suggested that iron smelting reached the region from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, perhaps by way of Meroe, but it may equally have grown locally out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper-smelting">copper smelting</a>, in which iron ore was used as a flux. The beehive and cylindrical furnaces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> differ in form from those of North Africa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and the iron workers of Taruga seem to have pre-heated the air entering the furnace to reach higher temperatures.</p><p>A short blade of around the 4th century BCE was probably made by piling: smelted fragments were wrapped in clay, heated to 1200 °C, taken from the fire and forged into a single piece, a method that limited oxidation during the long heating. Its metal is unusually free of impurities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taruga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Taruga</b> and Samun Dukiya, two sites where sculptures lay undisturbed, gave early radiocarbon and thermoluminescence dates, and older accounts usually placed the culture between about 500 BCE and 200 CE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nok-culture">Nok culture · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
